  [38;2;28;33;40;1mContainer network[m[38;2;28;33;40;1m namespaces[m                                                  
                                                                                
  [38;2;28;33;40mA network namespace isolates a process's view of the network: its[m[38;2;28;33;40m own[m         
  [38;2;28;33;40m[m[38;2;28;33;40minterfaces, routing table and firewall rules. Containers typically get[m[38;2;28;33;40m one[m    
  [38;2;28;33;40m[m[38;2;28;33;40meach, connected to the host through a virtual ethernet[m[38;2;28;33;40m pair.[m                  
                                                                                
  [38;2;28;33;40;1mHow it differs from a host[m[38;2;28;33;40;1m network[m                                            
                                                                                
  • [38;2;28;33;40mThe container sees only [m[38;2;88;98;112meth0[m[38;2;28;33;40m inside its own[m[38;2;28;33;40m namespace.[m                      
  • [38;2;28;33;40mThe host sees the other end of the veth pair, usually named like [m[38;2;88;98;112mveth1a2b3c[m[38;2;28;33;40m.[m
  • [38;2;28;33;40mTraffic between namespaces is routed the same as traffic between[m[38;2;28;33;40m hosts.[m     
                                                                                
